                            <p></p><h3>Definition of "vf14":</h3><p>The <b>VF-14</b> &#8220;steel&#8221; vacuum tube, manufactured by Telefunken, was used in the original <a href="http://recordinghacks.com/microphones/Neumann/U-47">U-47</a> and U-48 microphones. Because this tube is considered critical to achieving the U-47 sound, and because all the known replacements and recreations do not provide the audio magic that the original does, NOS Telefunken VF14s are rare and expensive.</p><p><img src="/images/tubes/VF14_tube.jpg" width="150" /></p><p>The best VF14 tubes, selected at the Neumann factory to meet noise and other specifications were <a href="http://recforums.prosoundweb.com/index.php/m/267670/0/">stamped with an &#8216;M&#8217;</a> (for <em>Mikrofon</em>) in white ink on the end of the tube. Non-conforming tubes were returned to the Telefunken factory for use in hi-fi equipment.</p><blockquote><p><cite>Oliver Archut</cite></p><p>Only 27,500 VF14 tubes were ever made by the original Telefunken company in Germany, and they stopped production in 1958.  Only about 1/3 of the VF14&#8217;s passed Neumann&#8217;s rigorous standards for being considered &#8220;microphone grade.&#8221; So, with about 9000 tubes and approximately 5500 U47/U48 mics made, there are virtually no spare tubes left.</p></blockquote><p>Read more about the <a href="http://www.wagner-microphones.com/vf14_background.htm">History of the VF14 tube</a> from Gunter Wagner. See also his notes about <a href="http://wagner-microphones.com/fake-VF14.htm">counterfeit VF-14 tubes</a>.</p>
